मण्डलम् सेतुः ॥   ॰ setuh — THE CALLER HALF: one cross-module call, and nothing else

॰ THE ANSWER IS ४२. `पारम्ॱद्विगुणय २१` — and the call across the module
॰ boundary is the only thing in this file's BODY that `निजम्.t1` beside it does
॰ not do. It is not the only thing in the FILE: this module also carries
॰ `आयातः पारम् ।`, which `निजम्.t1` has no need of and which — see ANSWERED
॰ 2026-09-21 below — is the line whose absence made the first four runs refuse.
॰
॰ WHAT THIS PAIR IS FOR. `W-279`'s ladder has answered five constructs natively
॰ — a constant, a CALL, a BRANCH, a LOOP and RECURSION (rung 100, status १०१७६,
॰ 1990 trace records byte-identical). Every one of those five reads
॰ `0 cross-module`, and the corpus places 1,105 cross-module calls. The compiled
॰ emitter has never once been asked for one, and no existing `spec/demo/` source
॰ can supply it, because a cross-module call needs a second module. This is that
॰ second module.
॰
॰ MEASURED 2026-09-21, AND THE RUNG CANNOT YET BE WRITTEN — FOUR RUNS, TWO OF
॰ THEM CONTROLS, AND THE CONTROLS ARE WHAT MAKE IT A FINDING RATHER THAN A
॰ COMPLAINT. All on tree `8c444afa`, release, via `tools/t1-image.sh
॰ --compiler crates/sadhana-t1/src` and `t1_boot`:
॰
॰   1  THE PAIR        --entry सेतुः मुख्य  पारम्.t1 सेतुः.t1
॰                      -> build: 1 source(s) failed to compile (सेतुः),
॰                         2 object(s) linked;  STOPPED AT link: empty image
॰   2  ORDER CONTROL   the same two sources with the CALLER listed FIRST
॰                      -> identical, to the interpreter step. Not an ordering
॰                         fault in `मण्डलानिप्रतिबिम्बम्`'s collect-then-compile.
॰   3  CALL CONTROL    `निजम्.t1` beside the same callee — the identical
॰                      arithmetic written INLINE, no cross-module call
॰                      -> build: 0 source(s) failed, 3 object(s) linked, and
॰                         `yantra-run` on the image halts with status ४२.
॰                         So the cross-module call is the only difference.
॰   4  CALLER CONTROL  `t1_boot --object` on `crates/sadhana-t1/src/utsarjana.t1`
॰                      — a CORPUS module that makes cross-module calls, through
॰                      the SAME path
॰                      -> object: उत्सर्जन text 454891 object 86552 records true
॰                         So a cross-module call is NOT broken in general.
॰
॰ THE STEP COUNTS ARE IN THE CYCLE LOG AND NOT IN THIS MARGIN, ON PURPOSE. The
॰ interpreter lexes this file, so quoting a step count HERE changes it — the
॰ first draft of this margin read 40,912,065 and the committed text measures
॰ 41,235,201 for the same build. A figure that its own statement moves is not a
॰ figure. `.loop/log/2026-09.tsv` carries both, against the tree that produced
॰ them.
॰
॰ WHERE IT STOPS, BY THE PRODUCT'S OWN CODE AND NOT BY INFERENCE. `t1_boot
॰ --object` on a caller outside the collected corpus, calling a REAL corpus
॰ routine (`अष्टकॱमुद्रणम् ६५`), dumps `सङ्कलनविरामभेद = Int(2)` =
॰ `सङ्कलनानिर्णयभेद` (`shrinkhala.t1:125`): **`अर्थ` refused it** — resolve or
॰ typecheck, NOT the emitter and NOT the assembler. Beside it in the same dump:
॰ `सञ्चयसिद्धमस्ति = true`, `निर्णायकसिद्धः = true`, `असङ्गृहीतमण्डलसंख्या = 0`,
॰ `असदस्यमस्ति = false` — the registry reports itself full and names no missing
॰ member, and the stage refuses anyway. The Rust-hosted chain says the same in
॰ words, with the store NOT pre-filled: `tools/demo.sh spec/rung/सेतुः.t1`
॰ reports `3 RESOLVE REFUSED — `पारम्ॱद्विगुणय` has no declaration`,
॰ having lexed 14 tokens and parsed 1 routine.
॰
॰ ANSWERED 2026-09-21, AND THE CAUSE IS IN THIS FILE AND NOT IN `अर्थ`. The
॰ four runs above are all explained by ONE missing line: this module named
॰ `पारम्` and never imported it. `अर्थ`'s qualified-name arm (`artha.t1:2944`)
॰ says in its own margin what it checks — *"WHAT IS CHECKED IS THE IMPORT, NOT
॰ THE MARK ... `आयातः` is a declaration now, so the MODULE half is looked up
॰ exactly like any other name"* — and that lookup is `नामनिर्णयः`, which walks
॰ the CALLER'S SCOPE STACK (`निर्णायकः ॱ परिसराः`). Only an `आयातः` declaration
॰ puts a module name on that stack. So `घोषणासञ्चय` reading itself full
॰ (`सञ्चयसिद्धमस्ति = true`, `असङ्गृहीतमण्डलसंख्या = 0`) was never relevant to
॰ this lookup: the registry and the scope stack are two different stores, and
॰ the resolver's module half consults only the second BY DESIGN.
॰
॰ THAT IS ALSO WHY RUN 4 IS NOT THE COUNTEREXAMPLE IT LOOKED LIKE.
॰ `utsarjana.t1` carries `आयातः मध्यरूप ।` at its line 158; `सेतुः.t1` carried
॰ no `आयातः` at all. The difference between the two callers was never
॰ collection or registry membership — both were collected — it was the import
॰ line, which is exactly the first property the previous cycle's item 2 named
॰ as the cheapest cut.
॰
॰ MEASURED BOTH WAYS, ON TREE `205816cc`, BEFORE AND AFTER THE ONE-LINE ADD:
॰
॰   RUST TWIN   `tools/demo.sh` on this file
॰               before -> `3 RESOLVE REFUSED — `पारम्ॱद्विगुणय` at line 74 has
॰                          no declaration`, 14 tokens, 1 declaration
॰               after  -> `3 RESOLVE ok`, `4 TYPECHECK ok`, 17 tokens,
॰               2 declarations, 1 routine / 2 blocks / 2 instructions / 1 call,
॰                          20 lines of T0, TWINS AGREE at 1552 octets
॰   PRODUCT     `t1_boot --object` with `T1_BOOT_COLLECT=spec/rung/पारम्.t1`
॰   `.t1` CHAIN before -> `सङ्कलनविरामभेद = Int(2)`, `निर्णयविरामभेद = Int(1)`
॰                          = RESOLVE, and `no text for सेतुः`
॰               after  -> `object: सेतुः text 1537 object 1008 records false`
॰
॰ `records false` IS NOT A SECOND DEFECT, and it is stated here so the next
॰ cycle does not chase it: the INLINE control `निजम्.t1`, which links and halts
॰ ४२, also reports `records false` through the same path. `records true` on
॰ `उत्सर्जन` is that module's size, not its cross-module calls.
॰
॰ ASKED OF THE MACHINE 2026-09-21T16:22Z, ON TREE `2d116943`, AND THE ANSWER IS
॰ ४२. The pair links and runs end to end; the ladder's native column is no
॰ longer five constructs at `0 cross-module`. Four runs, release, and the last
